WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:04.652 --> 00:00:09.492 line:88% position:50% align:center
Lesson five: notifications,
communication and accountability.

2
00:00:10.519 --> 00:00:14.454 line:88% position:50% align:center
In this lesson, we'll see how the
platform keeps people informed,

3
00:00:14.464 --> 00:00:20.229 line:88% position:50% align:center
how SMS messages are sent, and how the
audit log keeps a lasting trail of actions.

4
00:00:22.185 --> 00:00:25.007 line:88% position:50% align:center
The platform communicates in two ways.

5
00:00:26.052 --> 00:00:29.141 line:88% position:50% align:center
In-app notifications
appear inside the platform,

6
00:00:29.151 --> 00:00:31.036 line:88% position:50% align:center
in the notification centre.

7
00:00:31.536 --> 00:00:36.884 line:88% position:50% align:center
Each one is addressed to a specific person, and
it's stored in the database until it's read.

8
00:00:37.919 --> 00:00:42.688 line:88% position:50% align:center
SMS messages are sent to mobile phones, so
people are informed even when they aren't

9
00:00:42.698 --> 00:00:43.798 line:88% position:50% align:center
signed in.

10
00:00:44.752 --> 00:00:49.189 line:88% position:50% align:center
Some events use both channels,
and some use SMS only.

11
00:00:49.689 --> 00:00:51.170 line:88% position:50% align:center
Let's look at each.

12
00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:58.293 line:88% position:50% align:center
Open Notifications from the sidebar, or
select the bell icon at the top of any page.

13
00:00:58.794 --> 00:01:02.658 line:88% position:50% align:center
When you have unread notifications,
the bell shows a count.

14
00:01:03.719 --> 00:01:07.003 line:88% position:50% align:center
Each notification has a
title, a short message,

15
00:01:07.013 --> 00:01:08.455 line:88% position:50% align:center
and when it arrived.

16
00:01:08.956 --> 00:01:13.262 line:88% position:50% align:center
Unread notifications are marked with
a green bar along the left edge.

17
00:01:14.319 --> 00:01:18.658 line:88% position:50% align:center
Here, the administrator has received
alerts for additional fund requests,

18
00:01:18.668 --> 00:01:20.878 line:88% position:50% align:center
and for submitted daily logs.

19
00:01:21.919 --> 00:01:26.434 line:88% position:50% align:center
Selecting a notification marks it as
read and opens the related project.

20
00:01:26.934 --> 00:01:30.525 line:88% position:50% align:center
You can also mark all your
notifications as read at once.

21
00:01:32.052 --> 00:01:35.225 line:88% position:50% align:center
Here's a map of the events
that trigger communication.

22
00:01:36.285 --> 00:01:39.634 line:88% position:50% align:center
When a team leader is created,
assigned to a project,

23
00:01:39.644 --> 00:01:42.486 line:88% position:50% align:center
or removed from one through
the project's edit form,

24
00:01:42.496 --> 00:01:44.445 line:88% position:50% align:center
they receive an SMS.

25
00:01:44.946 --> 00:01:48.171 line:88% position:50% align:center
The welcome message includes
their sign-in details.

26
00:01:49.219 --> 00:01:54.020 line:88% position:50% align:center
Direct messages from an administrator
arrive both in the app and by SMS.

27
00:01:55.052 --> 00:01:59.378 line:88% position:50% align:center
When a casual worker is added or removed,
the worker and the administrator are

28
00:01:59.388 --> 00:02:01.337 line:88% position:50% align:center
informed by SMS.

29
00:02:02.385 --> 00:02:06.125 line:88% position:50% align:center
When a team leader submits
a daily log, requests funds,

30
00:02:06.135 --> 00:02:12.186 line:88% position:50% align:center
or marks a project complete, the administrator
receives an in-app notification and an SMS.

31
00:02:13.219 --> 00:02:16.425 line:88% position:50% align:center
And when the administrator
approves or declines funds,

32
00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:21.471 line:88% position:50% align:center
or approves or returns a project, the
team leader is notified both ways.

33
00:02:23.119 --> 00:02:27.510 line:88% position:50% align:center
When an event triggers an SMS, the
platform prepares a short message,

34
00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.110 line:88% position:50% align:center
and converts the phone number to
the international Kenyan format.

35
00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:35.111 line:88% position:50% align:center
It then sends the message
to a Cloudflare Worker:

36
00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:40.664 line:88% position:50% align:center
a small, secure web service that sits
between the platform and the SMS provider.

37
00:02:41.719 --> 00:02:46.175 line:88% position:50% align:center
The Worker passes the message
to Mobilesasa, the SMS gateway,

38
00:02:46.185 --> 00:02:50.531 line:88% position:50% align:center
which delivers it to the recipient's
phone using the configured sender ID.

39
00:02:51.585 --> 00:02:55.872 line:88% position:50% align:center
If the phone number is missing or
invalid, or the service can't be reached,

40
00:02:55.882 --> 00:02:59.420 line:88% position:50% align:center
the message is marked as failed
rather than silently ignored.

41
00:03:00.919 --> 00:03:04.392 line:88% position:50% align:center
Administrators configure SMS under Settings.

42
00:03:04.893 --> 00:03:09.414 line:88% position:50% align:center
The SMS Notifications panel shows
whether the system is in Live mode,

43
00:03:09.424 --> 00:03:10.996 line:88% position:50% align:center
or in Dry-run mode.

44
00:03:12.019 --> 00:03:15.486 line:88% position:50% align:center
The Worker URL is the address
of the Cloudflare Worker,

45
00:03:15.496 --> 00:03:19.450 line:88% position:50% align:center
and the Sender ID is the name that
appears on recipients' phones.

46
00:03:19.951 --> 00:03:22.030 line:88% position:50% align:center
These are technical settings.

47
00:03:22.529 --> 00:03:26.373 line:88% position:50% align:center
We've hidden the address here, and they
should only be changed on the advice of your

48
00:03:26.383 --> 00:03:27.695 line:88% position:50% align:center
technical team.

49
00:03:28.752 --> 00:03:31.678 line:88% position:50% align:center
Dry-run mode is a safe testing switch.

50
00:03:32.179 --> 00:03:36.335 line:88% position:50% align:center
When it's on, messages are recorded
in the platform's SMS list,

51
00:03:36.345 --> 00:03:38.217 line:88% position:50% align:center
but they're not actually sent.

52
00:03:39.619 --> 00:03:44.843 line:88% position:50% align:center
Under Auto-fire per event, each type of
automatic SMS can be switched on or off

53
00:03:44.853 --> 00:03:49.648 line:88% position:50% align:center
individually — for example, notifying
a team leader of a project assignment,

54
00:03:49.658 --> 00:03:52.754 line:88% position:50% align:center
or notifying the administrator
of each daily log.

55
00:03:53.819 --> 00:03:57.123 line:88% position:50% align:center
These switches control SMS messages only.

56
00:03:57.623 --> 00:04:00.419 line:88% position:50% align:center
In-app notifications are still created.

57
00:04:01.452 --> 00:04:06.260 line:88% position:50% align:center
One important detail: these SMS preferences
are saved in the web browser you're

58
00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:11.197 line:88% position:50% align:center
using. If an administrator signs
in on another computer or browser,

59
00:04:11.207 --> 00:04:13.768 line:88% position:50% align:center
the settings there need
to be checked separately.

60
00:04:14.819 --> 00:04:20.043 line:88% position:50% align:center
Below the switches, Save keeps your
changes to the Worker URL and Sender ID,

61
00:04:20.053 --> 00:04:24.685 line:88% position:50% align:center
and Test SMS sends a test message to
confirm that everything is working.

62
00:04:25.752 --> 00:04:29.700 line:88% position:50% align:center
The Recent SMS list then shows
each message's recipient,

63
00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:34.147 line:88% position:50% align:center
content and status:
sent, failed, or dry run.

64
00:04:34.647 --> 00:04:38.303 line:88% position:50% align:center
It covers messages sent from the
current browser session only.

65
00:04:39.719 --> 00:04:42.034 line:88% position:50% align:center
Now let's turn to accountability.

66
00:04:42.534 --> 00:04:47.433 line:88% position:50% align:center
The Audit Log, available to administrators,
records the important actions taken in

67
00:04:47.443 --> 00:04:48.543 line:88% position:50% align:center
the system.

68
00:04:49.519 --> 00:04:53.038 line:88% position:50% align:center
Each entry shows when the action
happened, who performed it,

69
00:04:53.048 --> 00:04:56.866 line:88% position:50% align:center
what the action was, and the
details — such as an amount,

70
00:04:56.876 --> 00:04:58.943 line:88% position:50% align:center
a project, or a person.

71
00:04:59.985 --> 00:05:04.285 line:88% position:50% align:center
Here, we can see an expense being
added, a project being created,

72
00:05:04.295 --> 00:05:07.840 line:88% position:50% align:center
funds being requested and
approved, a daily log submitted,

73
00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:09.826 line:88% position:50% align:center
and a casual worker added.

74
00:05:10.852 --> 00:05:14.416 line:88% position:50% align:center
Entries are written automatically
as people use the system.

75
00:05:14.917 --> 00:05:18.123 line:88% position:50% align:center
There's no option in the
platform to edit or delete them,

76
00:05:18.133 --> 00:05:21.410 line:88% position:50% align:center
and the page shows the most
recent one hundred entries.

77
00:05:22.452 --> 00:05:27.670 line:88% position:50% align:center
The dashboard's Recent Activity panel shows
the latest six entries from the same log.

78
00:05:29.219 --> 00:05:33.981 line:88% position:50% align:center
It's worth being clear about the difference
between a notification and an audit record.

79
00:05:35.019 --> 00:05:38.401 line:88% position:50% align:center
A notification is a message
to a specific person.

80
00:05:38.901 --> 00:05:42.921 line:88% position:50% align:center
Its purpose is to prompt action:
something needs your attention.

81
00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:46.164 line:88% position:50% align:center
Once it's read, its job is done.

82
00:05:47.219 --> 00:05:50.900 line:88% position:50% align:center
An audit record is a lasting
entry in the system's history.

83
00:05:51.401 --> 00:05:54.763 line:88% position:50% align:center
Its purpose is accountability:
it shows who did what,

84
00:05:54.773 --> 00:05:57.778 line:88% position:50% align:center
and when, so the record
can be reviewed later.

85
00:05:58.819 --> 00:06:02.689 line:88% position:50% align:center
Together, they make sure that the right
people are informed in the moment,

86
00:06:02.699 --> 00:06:06.849 line:88% position:50% align:center
and that the organisation can always
reconstruct what happened afterwards.

87
00:06:08.685 --> 00:06:14.704 line:88% position:50% align:center
To recap: in-app notifications prompt
action, SMS reaches people wherever they are,

88
00:06:14.714 --> 00:06:17.393 line:88% position:50% align:center
and the audit log keeps a lasting record.

89
00:06:18.419 --> 00:06:22.836 line:88% position:50% align:center
Administrators can control which automatic
SMS messages are sent and test the

90
00:06:22.846 --> 00:06:26.391 line:88% position:50% align:center
service safely, and everyone
should keep phone numbers accurate,

91
00:06:26.401 --> 00:06:28.871 line:88% position:50% align:center
so that messages reach the right people.

92
00:06:29.919 --> 00:06:34.232 line:88% position:50% align:center
In the final lesson, we'll bring everything
together with system administration,

93
00:06:34.242 --> 00:06:37.650 line:88% position:50% align:center
the platform's architecture,
and best practices.

94
00:06:40.619 --> 00:06:44.639 line:88% position:50% align:center
This training experience was
produced by KEYPOLE TECHNOLOGIES.
